## Engineering outcome observed after the ticket step

The course's engineering update for #4471 says **closed 05 oct**:

| Problem | Outcome | Engineering reason | Our ticket |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| parser | fixed | `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | #1 closed as completed |
| hint | won't fix | `booking-v3 form retired` | #2 closed as not planned |

Source: the authenticated [S01E03 challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows),
checked 1 November 2026 after the course accepted the ticket step. The course called
that step “Two tickets, both real” and required closure after #4471's work finished.
The two closure reasons are different: retiring a form is not an implemented hint fix.

The timing investigation #3 is withdrawn as unconfirmed, not marked fixed or counted
as an accepted third resolution. The update gives no technical outcome for its five
symptoms. Their bodies, attribution and uncertainty remain in the evidence below.
The former separate premature-invite ticket #4 is also withdrawn into #3. Withdrawal
is not completion. These closure outcomes supersede the earlier pending/queued
language retained below as the export and review history.

## Context

The incident page calls #4471 one known parser issue with one fix queued. Its exported
components distinguish the shared parser from the booking form's date hint, but their
classifier never reads report bodies. The desk-noted timing reports expose its blind
spot. Neither a matching subject nor the classifier's label establishes a shared cause.

This review uses the KB export dated 4 October 2026. It is not a live view of today's
support queue. Deduplicate overlapping export pages by report ID before counting.
The original table computes its own classification from tenant and channel data;
browser locale and intake tags are not its classifier inputs. The reviewed split
below also accounts for evidence in the report bodies.

## Original exported classification — not the reviewed ticket split

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

This table is preserved as evidence of how the desk files reports. Its parser count
includes all desk-noted timing reports because `causeOf` only checks tenant locale,
form version and channel. Its “causes” heading is the component's label, not proof
that it explains every body. The desk note only lists IDs under `oneDayMove`; there
is no separate diagnostic note for each ID.

## Revised problems and tickets

<ReviewedProblems />

**Parser:** the shared parser reads numeric dates month first for day-first tenants.
Remove the desk-noted timing reports from its ticket count. The remaining group has
explicit reversed numeric date pairs plus contextual assignments R-41290 (asks for
day-first support), R-41417 (unspecified wrong day), R-41514 (dates-in-words workaround)
and R-41615 (October becomes January). The generic wrong-day and workaround reports
do not prove their mechanism; keep those assignments provisional. Fix the shared
parser to respect an explicit date format and exchange unambiguous dates.

**Hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form reads dates in the day-first
tenant's order. Correct the displayed hint to match the input interpretation. This
needs its own ticket and fix; a shared-parser fix alone does not fix the misleading
form. It affects form reports from Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour.

**Timing:** treat the desk-noted reports as one timing investigation, preserving
both day-discrepancy and invite-ordering symptom subcases. The course rejected the
prior separation of these subcases, stating “Two of yours are the same problem.”
This revision follows that feedback and the desk's complete `oneDayMove` grouping.
Neither grouping proves a shared implementation fault. Recover intended local
dates, event instants, IANA zones, all-day flags, serialized invite values and viewer
settings; correlate booking/message IDs and created, queued, sent and received
timestamps. Check clock skew and stale messages before accepting the reported
pre-booking arrival as a real ordering violation.

The reviewed report grouping was recorded in these tickets; the lifecycle outcomes above now apply:

- [1: Shared parser reads day-first tenant dates as month-first](https://github.com/zeta5461/megapublic/issues/1)
- [2: booking-v3 date hint contradicts day-first form parsing](https://github.com/zeta5461/megapublic/issues/2)
- [3: Investigate invite date/time and delivery-order discrepancies](https://github.com/zeta5461/megapublic/issues/3)

The former premature-invite ticket #4 is closed as withdrawn and incorporated
into #3. It is not a fourth accepted or completed problem.

<Callout kind="warn">
  The five desk-noted IDs are removed from the reviewed parser ticket. No report
  in this set uses the form channel, so a booking-v3 form hint is not supported
  for them. Tenant locale does not identify a time zone. The timing investigation
  preserves observed subcases without claiming an established root cause.
</Callout>

## Search for other reports with the same pattern

Every unique body was checked for numeric date reversals and normalized text patterns.
The numeric-pair check found explicit swaps; the non-matching bodies were read
individually. All normalized patterns were inspected for additional timing language.
No additional explicit one-day/location-dependent-day discrepancy or pre-booking
invite claim was found in this export. Generic wrong-day statements remain uncertain
contextual parser assignments, not proven exclusions of another cause. The audit
retains every report ID and its reviewed assignment in `report-audit.json`.

## What the queue shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived over months; the large merge spike is a desk action, not the start
of the incident. Reports continue after that spike. “Known” and “fix queued” are
workflow labels, not evidence of resolution. Keep receiving and classifying new
reports while engineering works; do not fold them into #4471 merely because the
subject matches.

The first desk-noted timing reports were received before the July bulk merge; the
last was still being merged into the same ticket in October. Queue receipt and
merge timestamps are not booking, event or delivery timestamps. They show how the
desk handled reports, not when the underlying invite was sent or why a date changed.

## Proposed decision

This record proposes replacing [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
and the subject-only rule in [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe). The accepted historical
decision remains unchanged. Desk acceptance of this replacement is still pending.

1. Read the report body, resolve the tenant, and check its locale, form version
   and channel. Use `causeOf` and `sameCause` as routing context, with
   `Intl.DateTimeFormat` for locale order. Check body evidence before accepting
   their result; neither function establishes the mechanism of a timing anomaly.
2. Merge only when the evidence supports the same cause. Different subjects can
   describe the same cause; identical subjects can describe different causes.
3. Unknown tenant or unplaced cause: keep apart and investigate. Do not treat two
   unknowns as duplicates. Preserve report IDs and provenance when moving reports.
4. Recompute ticket counts from unique report IDs. Preserve uncertain symptoms
   explicitly and revisit the classifier when investigation contradicts it.
5. Route parser, form-hint and timing reports to their respective tickets. Preserve
   timing subcases in the same investigation and verify the mechanism and fixes
   before marking reports resolved.

## Check the rule against examples

<DedupeExplorer />

The rendered initial pair and case table exercise the exported locale/form logic;
they do not exercise report-body interpretation. This saved
HTML is a static render; changing the form controls requires a hydrated app.

## Consequences

Triage takes more reading than a subject-only merge, but keeps distinct fixes and
their impact visible. The existing incident narrative and old triage rule should
be updated by the desk when it accepts this proposal. Do not silently rewrite an
accepted decision or present this proposal as already adopted.

## Sources and verification

The export's incident page supplies the “known” narrative; `reports-4471.json`
supplies reports and timestamps; tenant JSON files supply locale and form version;
`causes.ts` and `locales.ts` determine the original classifier; `desk-notes.json`
identifies reports the desk listed as `oneDayMove`. Reviewed problem annotations
in `report-review.json` separate their body evidence without changing the exported
classifier. `ReviewedProblems.svelte` computes the revised counts and report evidence
view. The original cause table, timeline, example table and desk reference were
rendered from the supplied components, not copied as hand-entered results.
Course acceptance remains pending.

